          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong Lie group
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+structure"} smooth structure
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LieGrp"} LieGrp
          %h2#definition Definition
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong Lie group
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
              whose
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} underlying
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elements"} elements
              is equipped with the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
              such that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magma"} group multiplication
              and
              = succeed "-assigning" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse"} inverse
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
              are
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            %p
              In other words, a Lie group is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+object"} group object
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal to
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SmthMfd"} SmthMfd
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
          %p
            Usually the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            is assumed to be defined over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            and to be of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+number"} finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            (f.d.), but extensions of the definition to some other
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+fields"} ground fields
            or to -
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite-dimensional+manifolds"} infinite-dimensional manifolds
            are also relevant, sometimes under other names (such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9chet+Lie+group"} Fréchet Lie group
            when the underlying manifold is an infinite-dimensional
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9chet+manifold"} Fréchet manifold
          %p
            A real Lie group is called a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+Lie+group"} compact Lie group
            (or
            = succeed "," do
              %em connected
            %em simply connected
            Lie group, etc) if its underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
            (or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+space"} connected
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simply+connected+space"} simply connected
          %p
            Every connected finite dimensional real Lie group is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphic
            to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+Lie+group"} compact Lie group
            (its
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maximal+compact+subgroup"} maximal compact subgroup
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean space
          %p
            Every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian
            connected compact finite dimensional real Lie group is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torus"} torus
            (a product of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circles"} circles
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ×
                    %mi …
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^n = S^1\times S^1 \times \ldots \times S^1
          %p
            There is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+space"} infinitesimal
            version of a Lie group, a so-called
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lie+group"} local Lie group
          %p
            The first order
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object"} infinitesimal
            approximation to a Lie group is its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#lies_three_theorems Lie’s three theorems
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sophus+Lie"} Sophus Lie
            proved several theorems, known as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie%27s+three+theorems"} Lie's three theorems
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebras"} Lie algebras
            and Lie groups. Lie’s third theorem is about the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
            of finite-dimensional real Lie algebras and local Lie groups. Because
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%89lie+Cartan"} Élie Cartan
            extended this to a global integrability theorem, Lie’s third theorem is also called the Cartan-Lie theorem.
          %h3#lie_subgroups Lie subgroups
          %p
          #CartanClosedSubgroupTheorem.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan%27s+closed+subgroup+theorem"}> Cartan's closed subgroup theorem
                )
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \subset G
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+subgroup"} closed subgroup
              of a (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+number"} finite
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension+of+a+manifold"} dimensional
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              is a sub-Lie group, hence a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/submanifold"} submanifold
              such that its group operations are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
              with respect to the the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/submanifold"} submanifold
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+structure"} smooth structure
          %h3#classification Classification
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
            %p
              Every connected finite-dimensional real Lie group is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphic
              to a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+Lie+group"} compact Lie group
              and a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Euclidean space
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torus"} torus
              (a product of circles
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi T
                        %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ×
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ×
                      %mi …
                      %mo ×
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^n = S^1\times S^1 \times \ldots \times S^1
            %p
          %p
            The
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+Lie+group"} simple Lie group
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+Lie+group"}> classical Lie group
              s
          %p and a finite snumber o
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+Lie+group"}> exceptional Lie group
              s
          %h3#different_lie_group_structures_on_a_group Different Lie group structures on a group
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            a bare
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            (without smooth structure) there may be more than one way to equip it with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stuff%2C+structure%2C+property"} structure
            of a Lie group.
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              As bare
              = succeed "s," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
              are, for all
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+number"} rational number
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
              whose
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinality"} cardinality
              of the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum"} continuum
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mn 2
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi ℵ
                            %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^{\aleph_0}
            %p
              Therefore these are all
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphic"} isomorphic
              as bare group. But equipped with their canonical Lie group structure (as in the
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#Examples"} Examples
          %h3#different_topologies_on_a_lie_group Different topologies on a Lie group
          %ul
            %li
              Linus Kramer,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The topology of a simple Lie group is essentially unique
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1009.5457"} arXiv
          %blockquote
            %p
              Abstract: We study locally compact group topologies on simple Lie groups. We show that the Lie group topology on such a group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              is very rigid: every ‘abstract’ isomorphism between
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              and a locally compact and
              = succeed "-compact" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              is automatically a homeomorphism, provided that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              is absolutely simple. If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              is complex, then non-continuous field automorphisms of the complex numbers have to be considered, but that is all.
          %h3#which_topological_groups_admit_lie_group_structure Which topological groups admit Lie group structure?
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert%27s+fifth+problem"} Hilbert's fifth problem
          %h3#relation_to_topological_groups Relation to topological groups
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+homomorphisms+of+Lie+groups+are+smooth"} continuous homomorphisms of Lie groups are smooth
          %h3#homotopy_groups Homotopy groups
          %p
            List of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
            of the manifolds underlying the classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groups"} Lie groups
            are for instance in (
            %a{:href => "#Abanov09"}> Abanov 09
            ).
          %h2#applications Applications
          %h3#in_differential_geometry In differential geometry
          %p
            A central concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            is that of a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to X
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
